WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.280
Take a healthy young man of about 70 kilograms.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.360
At rest, he takes up about 250 millilitres of oxygen per minute.

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.700
At the end of a maximal run, he may take up ten or more times as much.

4
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:20.560
That oxygen passes through the lungs, the heart, the blood and the capillaries

5
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.560
before a mitochondrion uses it.

6
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.340
Which link sets the ceiling?

7
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:28.573
Fick's principle splits the rise into two factors.

8
00:00:29.253 --> 00:00:34.333
Oxygen uptake equals cardiac output times the oxygen extracted from each litre of

9
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:40.273
blood. That extraction is arterial oxygen content minus mixed venous oxygen content,

10
00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:42.913
in millilitres of oxygen per litre of blood.

11
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:47.913
Take illustrative numbers at rest: cardiac output is five litres per minute, and

12
00:00:47.913 --> 00:00:49.933
extraction is 50 millilitres per litre.

13
00:00:50.493 --> 00:00:53.533
Five times 50 gives 250 millilitres per minute.

14
00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:58.347
Now the same man at maximal exercise, with illustrative numbers.

15
00:00:59.047 --> 00:01:04.927
Heart rate is 195 beats per minute, and stroke volume is 100 millilitres per beat.

16
00:01:05.627 --> 00:01:10.947
Cardiac output reaches 19.5 litres per minute, a rise of 3.9 times.

17
00:01:11.427 --> 00:01:16.867
Extraction rises to 160 millilitres per litre, a rise of 3.2 times.

18
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:21.327
Together they make oxygen uptake 12.5 times its resting value.

19
00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:23.787
Which factor sets the ceiling?

20
00:01:24.507 --> 00:01:29.907
Andersen and Saltin had five healthy subjects kick one leg, so the active muscle was

21
00:01:29.907 --> 00:01:31.667
essentially one quadriceps.

22
00:01:32.107 --> 00:01:36.947
Flow to that leg rose with workload to an average of 5.7 litres per minute.

23
00:01:37.487 --> 00:01:41.867
They estimated maximal perfusion at about two and a half litres per kilogram of

24
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:43.107
active muscle per minute.

25
00:01:43.707 --> 00:01:45.807
This did not test whole body exercise.

26
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.620
Suppose, with made up values, that running uses 20 kilograms of muscle.

27
00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.680
Eighty percent of the heart's 19.5 litres per minute reaches it.

28
00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.900
If the one leg rate applies to all of that muscle, it could accept 50 litres per

29
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.680
minute. The heart sends 15.6.

30
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.400
Muscle could accept three times what the heart delivers, so in this example the

31
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.400
heart sets the ceiling.

32
00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:14.543
In healthy people doing whole body exercise at sea level, the evidence points to

33
00:02:14.543 --> 00:02:17.323
oxygen delivery, mainly cardiac output.

34
00:02:17.823 --> 00:02:21.723
One training study's authors report that returning red cell volume to baseline

35
00:02:21.723 --> 00:02:25.443
brought peak oxygen uptake back to its pretraining level.

36
00:02:25.863 --> 00:02:30.683
But removing blood also lowers blood volume and preload, so it cannot separate

37
00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.923
oxygen-carrying capacity from volume.

38
00:02:33.443 --> 00:02:37.283
A later study found the gain preserved, so the result is not settled.

39
00:02:37.283 --> 00:02:43.343
Oxygen uptake is flow times extraction, and in whole body exercise at sea level the

40
00:02:43.343 --> 00:02:44.943
evidence points mainly to flow.
